<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasion of Java</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1292">1292</a>–<a href="/years/1293">1293</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> was a naval expedition sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> in 1292 to 1293. The Javanese prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a> used the Mongol force against his rival and then turned on the invaders, driving them out. He went on to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-java.jpg" alt="Mongol invasion of Java" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Akinneyfluorse · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_invasion_of_java_(map).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> had sent envoys demanding tribute, and when his envoy Meng-qi reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singhasari">Singhasari</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kertanegara">Kertanegara</a> branded his face and cut his ears. Kublai ordered a punitive expedition in 1292, hoping also to control the sea trade routes of the archipelago. According to the History of Yuan, 20,000–30,000 men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguang">Huguang</a> sailed in 500–1,000 ships under Shi Bi, Yighmish and Gao Xing. Meanwhile, with Singhasari's army away in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> revolted with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-wiraraja">Arya Wiraraja</a> and killed Kertanegara. Kertanegara's son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a> submitted to Jayakatwang and was allowed to settle the Tarik timberland, which he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The fleet sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, and on 22 January 1293 Yighmish went ahead with the emperor's order. When the force landed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Wijaya</a> offered his submission and a tribute including two princesses in return for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a>. The Yuan troops gathered on the Kali Mas on 22 March and beat off an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>'s army on 15 April. On 26 April they stormed Daha, where some 5,000 Kediri soldiers were killed and thousands more drowned, and that afternoon Jayakatwang surrendered.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raden-wijaya">Wijaya</a> then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, ostensibly to prepare his tribute, and asked that the Yuan escort come unarmed. On 26 May he ambushed the two hundred soldiers sent to him and attacked the main camp, cutting Shi Bi off from his army. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> winds about to end, the Yuan forces killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayakatwang">Jayakatwang</a> and his son and sailed for home on 31 May. Modern estimates put their losses at 12,000–18,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On their return Shi Bi received seventy lashes and lost a third of his property, and Yighmish was reprimanded and lost a third of his, though both were later pardoned; Gao Xing was rewarded for saving the army from total disaster. The expedition was the last of Kublai's reign, and a further invasion with a 100,000-strong army was dropped after his death. The campaign may have brought the first use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> to the archipelago, and Javanese shipbuilders took up Chinese techniques such as iron nails, watertight bulkheads and a central rudder.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol invasion of Java” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
